\section{Where the maximum is}{


The \code{at_max} attribute holds, for each series, the scanned value at which
that series' measured quantity is largest. On a yield-versus-fishing-mortality
scan that is \eqn{F_{MSY}}; on a scan over fishing effort it is the effort
that maximises the quantity being plotted. \code{max_value} holds the value
attained there.

This is the largest value \strong{among those that were scanned}, not the maximum
of the underlying curve. It is therefore only as good as the grid you gave in
\code{scan_values}, and the way to sharpen it is to scan a finer grid near the
maximum, not to interpolate a coarse one. Subsetting a \code{MizerScan} with \code{[}
recomputes both attributes from the rows that remain, so they never go stale.
}

\section{Limitations}{


Because the object is a data frame subclass, its attributes survive base R
subsetting with \code{[} but are dropped by functions that rebuild the data frame,
including \code{dplyr::filter()}, \code{dplyr::mutate()}, \code{subset()} and \code{transform()}.
A scan that has lost its attributes can no longer be plotted. Subset with \code{[},
or rebuild the object with \code{MizerScan()}.
}

